(int), PLUS, MINUS, STAR, SLASH, LPAREN, RPAREN, EOF.\n- Define arithmetic grammar; semantic actions compute and return an int directly.\n- Entry point `start` of type int: use `%start start` and `%type <int> start`;\n  `start` parses an `expr` followed by EOF.\n- Enforce: * / bind tighter than + -; all operators left-to-right associative\n  (10-3-2 = 5); parentheses override.\nAfter writing, show `cat calc/Parser.fsy`. Do not build yet.\nSuccess criterion: Parser.fsy declares correct tokens, entry point `start`, and\nenforces precedence/associativity.\n\nStep 4 \u2014 Write Program.fs:\nAfter Step 3, all source files exist. Rewrite calc/Program.fs \u2014 the CLI entry point.\nProgram.fs must:\n- Read exactly one command-line argument (the expression string).\n- If wrong number of args, print usage to stderr and exit nonzero.\n- Open `FSharp.Text.Lexing` (NOT Microsoft.FSharp.Text.Lexing \u2014 does not exist in\n  .NET 10).\n- Create LexBuffer<char> from the argument using LexBuffer<char>.FromString.\n- Call `Parser.start Lexer.tokenize lexbuf` to parse and evaluate.\n- Print the integer result (only the result) to stdout followed by a newline.\nAfter writing, show `cat calc/Program.fs`. Do not build yet.\nSuccess criterion: Program.fs reads argv.[0], parses with generated modules, prints\nthe integer result.\n\nStep 5 \u2014 Build and test:\nAfter Step 4, calc/ has all four source files. Build and verify all canonical tests.\nBuild: cd calc && dotnet build 2>&1\nIf build fails, read the error, fix the relevant source file (bash heredoc/printf/tee\nonly \u2014 NOT file_editor), and rebuild. Repeat until build succeeds. Report each error\nand what was changed to fix it.\nRun all three canonical tests and report exact outputs + exit codes:\n  dotnet run -- \"2+3*4\"\n  dotnet run -- \"(2+3)*4\"\n  dotnet run -- \"10-3-2\"\nRequired outputs: 14 / 20 / 5 respectively.\nReport the EXACT output and exit code for each test. If a test prints the wrong\nnumber, state that c