{"id": "9c3b3193-86cb-49fe-af2b-a0f02094db79", "timestamp": "2026-06-02T11:20:28.205583", "source": "agent", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"thought": "Interesting! Now the error is \"Unexpected character '%'\" on line 1. This means FsLex in version 11.3.0 does NOT use the `%{ ... %}` header block syntax. It seems like FsLex 11.3.0 uses a different syntax.\n\nLet me check: In FsLexYacc 11.x, the syntax might have changed. The `%{ ... %}` block is the traditional ocamllex/FsLex syntax. But maybe in version 11.3.0, FsLex uses a different approach.\n\nActually, looking at the FsLexYacc 11.x documentation, it seems like the lexer file format might have changed. In newer versions, FsLex might use a simpler format without the `%{ ... %}` block.\n\nLet me try putting the `open` statements directly at the top without any block, and see if the issue was something else.\n\nWait, the first error was \"parse error\" on line 1 with `open Parser`. The second error is \"Unexpected character '%'\" on line 1 with `%{`. \n\nLet me look at the actual FsLexYacc 11.3.0 source to understand the lexer file format. Actually, let me just try a minimal approach \u2014 no `open` at all, and see what happens.\n\nActually, I think the issue might be that FsLex 11.3.0 uses a completely different file format. Let me check if it uses the `%%` separator or something else.\n\nLooking at the FsLexYacc GitHub repo, in version 11.x, the lexer file format should still be the traditional format. But the issue might be that `open` is not a valid keyword in the lexer file.\n\nLet me try a different approach: put the `open` statements inside the rule body using a different syntax, or use fully qualified names.\n\nActually, I think the real issue is that FsLex doesn't support `open` statements at all in the lexer file. The generated code just needs the token type definition and the rule. The `open` statements should be in the generated .fs file or in the Program.fs.\n\nLet me try without any `open` statements and use fully qualified names.", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "4eaaf29f-386b-4d96-b67b-ef38d87a27ee", "tool_call": {"id": "4eaaf29f-386b-4d96-b67b-ef38d87a27ee", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"thought\": \"Interesting! Now the error is \\\"Unexpected character '%'\\\" on line 1. This means FsLex in version 11.3.0 does NOT use the `%{ ... %}` header block syntax. It seems like FsLex 11.3.0 uses a different syntax.\\n\\nLet me check: In FsLexYacc 11.x, the syntax might have changed. The `%{ ... %}` block is the traditional ocamllex/FsLex syntax. But maybe in version 11.3.0, FsLex uses a different approach.\\n\\nActually, looking at the FsLexYacc 11.x documentation, it seems like the lexer file format might have changed. In newer versions, FsLex might use a simpler format without the `%{ ... %}` block.\\n\\nLet me try putting the `open` statements directly at the top without any block, and see if the issue was something else.\\n\\nWait, the first error was \\\"parse error\\\" on line 1 with `open Parser`. The second error is \\\"Unexpected character '%'\\\" on line 1 with `%{`. \\n\\nLet me look at the actual FsLexYacc 11.3.0 source to understand the lexer file format. Actually, let me just try a minimal approach \\u2014 no `open` at all, and see what happens.\\n\\nActually, I think the issue might be that FsLex 11.3.0 uses a completely different file format. Let me check if it uses the `%%` separator or something else.\\n\\nLooking at the FsLexYacc GitHub repo, in version 11.x, the lexer file format should still be the traditional format. But the issue might be that `open` is not a valid keyword in the lexer file.\\n\\nLet me try a different approach: put the `open` statements inside the rule body using a different syntax, or use fully qualified names.\\n\\nActually, I think the real issue is that FsLex doesn't support `open` statements at all in the lexer file. The generated code just needs the token type definition and the rule. The `open` statements should be in the generated .fs file or in the Program.fs.\\n\\nLet me try without any `open` statements and use fully qualified names.\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-9e88b463-b5ed-471a-8ecf-f89b91de9ed8", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "think: {\"thought\": \"Interesting! Now the error is \\\"Unexpected character '%'\\\" on line 1. This means FsLex in version 11.3.0 does NOT use the `%{ ... %}` header block syntax. It seems like FsLex 11.3.0 uses a different syntax.\\n\\nLet me check: In FsLexYacc 11.x, the syntax might have changed. The `%{ ... %}` block is the traditional ocamllex/FsLex syntax. But maybe in version 11.3.0, FsLex uses a different approach.\\n\\nActually, looking at the FsLexYacc 11.x documentation, it seems like the lexer file format might have changed. In newer versions, FsLex might use a simpler format without the `%{ ... %}` block.\\n\\nLet me try putting the `open` statements directly at the top without any block, and see if the issue was something else.\\n\\nWait, the first error was \\\"parse error\\\" on line 1 with `open Parser`. The second error is \\\"Unexpected character '%'\\\" on line 1 with `%{`. \\n\\nLet me look at the actual FsLexYacc 11.3.0 source to understand the lexer file format. Actually, let me just try a minimal approach \\u2014 no `open` at all, and see what happens.\\n\\nActually, I think the issue might be that FsLex 11.3.0 uses a completely different file format. Let me check if it uses the `%%` separator or something else.\\n\\nLooking at the FsLexYacc GitHub repo, in version 11.x, the lexer file format should still be the traditional format. But the issue might be that `open` is not a valid keyword in the lexer file.\\n\\nLet me try a different approach: put the `open` statements inside the rule body using a different syntax, or use 