{"id": "9b7b1823-80e6-40fb-a3b7-a9e405e3251b", "timestamp": "2026-06-02T11:21:38.886083", "source": "agent", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"thought": "Now I'm getting somewhere! The error is \"The macro n is not defined\". This means FsLex 11.x uses a different syntax for capturing matched text. Instead of `as n`, it might use a different syntax.\n\nIn traditional ocamllex/FsLex, you capture matched text with `as name`. But in FsLex 11.x, the syntax might be different. Let me check.\n\nActually, looking at the error \"The macro n is not defined\", it seems like FsLex 11.x uses a different syntax for capturing. Instead of `['0'-'9']+ as n`, it might use `['0'-'9']+ as n` but with a different way to reference it.\n\nWait, actually the error says \"The macro n is not defined\". This might mean that FsLex 11.x doesn't support the `as` syntax at all, or uses a different syntax.\n\nLet me try without the `as n` part and see what happens. In some lexer generators, you can access the matched text via a built-in variable.\n\nActually, looking at the FsLexYacc 11.x source code, I think the issue might be that FsLex 11.x uses a different syntax for capturing. Instead of `as n`, it might use `n` directly without the `as` keyword, or it might use a different approach.\n\nLet me try using `lexeme` or `lexeme_string` to get the matched text.", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "7e5aedda-fdee-4bc9-909e-5cea7976859d", "tool_call": {"id": "7e5aedda-fdee-4bc9-909e-5cea7976859d", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"thought\": \"Now I'm getting somewhere! The error is \\\"The macro n is not defined\\\". This means FsLex 11.x uses a different syntax for capturing matched text. Instead of `as n`, it might use a different syntax.\\n\\nIn traditional ocamllex/FsLex, you capture matched text with `as name`. But in FsLex 11.x, the syntax might be different. Let me check.\\n\\nActually, looking at the error \\\"The macro n is not defined\\\", it seems like FsLex 11.x uses a different syntax for capturing. Instead of `['0'-'9']+ as n`, it might use `['0'-'9']+ as n` but with a different way to reference it.\\n\\nWait, actually the error says \\\"The macro n is not defined\\\". This might mean that FsLex 11.x doesn't support the `as` syntax at all, or uses a different syntax.\\n\\nLet me try without the `as n` part and see what happens. In some lexer generators, you can access the matched text via a built-in variable.\\n\\nActually, looking at the FsLexYacc 11.x source code, I think the issue might be that FsLex 11.x uses a different syntax for capturing. Instead of `as n`, it might use `n` directly without the `as` keyword, or it might use a different approach.\\n\\nLet me try using `lexeme` or `lexeme_string` to get the matched text.\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-c3619c49-1969-4db0-afef-d5fe655c7e6a", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "think: {\"thought\": \"Now I'm getting somewhere! The error is \\\"The macro n is not defined\\\". This means FsLex 11.x uses a different syntax for capturing matched text. Instead of `as n`, it might use a different syntax.\\n\\nIn traditional ocamllex/FsLex, you capture matched text with `as name`. But in FsLex 11.x, the syntax might be different. Let me check.\\n\\nActually, looking at the error \\\"The macro n is not defined\\\", it seems like FsLex 11.x uses a different syntax for capturing. Instead of `['0'-'9']+ as n`, it might use `['0'-'9']+ as n` but with a different way to reference it.\\n\\nWait, actually the error says \\\"The macro n is not defined\\\". This might mean that FsLex 11.x doesn't support the `as` syntax at all, or uses a different syntax.\\n\\nLet me try without the `as n` part and see what happens. In some lexer generators, you can access the matched text via a built-in variable.\\n\\nActually, looking at the