st the FixLineDirectives target looking in the wrong place.\n\nThe simplest fix: set FsLexOutputFolder and FsYaccOutputFolder to the obj path, OR change the sed to look in the current directory.\n\nActually, the better approach is to set the output folders to the obj directory. Let me update the .fsproj to add the property definitions.\n\nWait, but there's another issue. The generated files are in the current directory, and the Compile items reference them without a path. If I change the output folder to obj/..., the Compile items need to reference the new path.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The simplest fix is:\n1. Set FsLexOutputFolder and FsYaccOutputFolder to the obj directory path\n2. Update the Compile items to reference the generated files from the obj directory\n\nOR:\n1. Keep the generated files in the current directory (don't set output folders)\n2. Fix the sed command to look in the current directory\n\nOption 2 is simpler. Let me update the .fsproj to fix the sed paths.\n\nActually, looking more carefully, the generated Lexer.fs is in the current directory. The sed command should look there. Let me just fix the sed paths to use the current directory.\n\nBut wait - the generated files might be in a different location depending on the build configuration. Let me check what the actual build output says about where files are generated.\n\nActually, the simplest and most robust approach: set FsLexOutputFolder and FsYaccOutputFolder to the obj directory, and update the Compile items to use the generated paths. This is the standard approach.\n\nLet me rewrite the .fsproj properly.\n", "kind": "ThinkAction"}, "tool_name": "think", "tool_call_id": "8d66d2f8-15df-4d4d-9abe-ea99239b349c", "tool_call": {"id": "8d66d2f8-15df-4d4d-9abe-ea99239b349c", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "think", "arguments": "{\"thought\": \"The issue is clear now:\\n1. `FsLexOutputFolder` and `FsYaccOutputFolder` are not set by default in FsLexYacc 11.3.0, so they default to 